Found in the k199 review round, probed on main through a test overlay. nestedScripts joins every token after the script flag into the nested script, but bash runs only the first as the script and takes the rest as $0, $1. So with deny Bash(reboot), bash -c reboot is denied and bash -c reboot x is allowed: an exact, no-star deny rule is passed by appending any word. k127 recorded the joining as erring toward more segments, which holds for prefix rules only.

Owner: unreagent-worker, with the failing table row first (skill unreagent-permissions, How to add a case). Same file as k127, so it runs after k127 lands. Done when: only the first token after the flag is split as the script, both senses pinned by rows.
